From 124aea2dff99fce4fd8ed205176b781f41ba1797 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 06:26:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] feat(#37): the EXCEPTION operator narrows a handler to an
 observed subtype or a tuple's remaining members

The defect, verbatim: `suite_edit.remove_definition_from_source` parses with `except Exception:`.
Detective #103 widened it from `except SyntaxError:`, because on 3.14 the parser also raises
UnicodeEncodeError on a lone surrogate. #103's regression test fed a lone surrogate; `audit` read it as
redundant for kills AND lines, and `--remove` deleted it. By the measurement that was correct: another
test covered the same lines and killed the same mutants. What the deleted test alone carried was the
handler's BREADTH (that it catches something other than SyntaxError), and no operator asks that question.
`handler_broaden` is the widening dual; narrowing is the refactor that lets an error escape that used to
be caught.

Why the obvious fix is wrong: a fixed invented subclass (`Exception` -> `ValueError`) is cheap and
arbitrary, it measures the operator's choice and not the program's, and it cannot be the `except
Exception` of #103 (a surrogate raises none of the usual picks). The ruling (D4) is OBSERVED narrowing:
a single-type handler narrows only to a strict subclass the baseline actually delivered to it, and a
tuple handler `except (A, B)` drops one member per mutant (static and exact, no observation needed).

The observation is `Wesker.caught_exceptions.observe_caught_exceptions(func_node, code)`: a
`sys.monitoring` EXCEPTION_HANDLED subscription through the broker (`monitoring.py` gains the global-only
event beside PY_UNWIND; no new trace hook, the ownership guard stays green). The event fires once per
`try` whose handler chain an exception enters, with the chain's entry offset, so `handler_entry_lines`
maps the offset to the first clause's line and `catching_clause` decides which clause caught it from the
declared names and the exception's MRO. Observed: {handler line: {module:qualname: MRO names}}, passed
as `observed_caught` to `generate_mutants`, `estimate_universe_size` and `run_function_profiling`
(the same channel as `observed_return_types`).

What each state of the pure decisions means. `catching_clause`: `clause:<i>` is the first clause that is
bare or names a class in the MRO, `none` is an exception that leaves the try (not observed as caught).
`narrowing_standing`: `drop_member` (tuple), `observed` (a strict subtype arrived), `no_strict_subtype`
(observed, and the handler only ever caught exactly its declared type: nothing to narrow to),
`unobserved` (no observation was made: the handler is WITHHELD by name and never read as clean),
`bare` / `opaque_type` (nothing to narrow). `narrowing_candidates` is the sorted strict subtypes. A type
from another module is reached as `__import__('mod').Qual`, not through the mutant's globals, which need
not hold the name. `except*` clauses are outside the question (their type is an ExceptionGroup).

Policy 10 (set by #53) gains the sub-mode: the corpus gained fp_exception_narrow (EXCEPTION 7 on it, the
tuple's three drop-ones; no existing row moved): one-sign 10.f7358643c7e0 -> 10.2598eed3d331, two-sign
10+neg.cad6fd2aabb8 -> 10+neg.34638cf3c016.

What was NOT closed: nothing in Wesker's own run calls the observation yet. Detective's baseline runs
the tests (`Detective/capture.py`, beside `capture_return_types`) and must wrap that run in
`observe_caught_exceptions` and hand the dict to `run_function_profiling(observed_caught=...)` and the
universe estimate; until it does, single-type handlers are withheld(unobserved_handler) and only tuple
drop-ones are generated (written up for the integrator). DOF budgeting (`dimension_budget`,
`dof_universe`) does not yet count the observed narrowings, so the DOF denominator undercounts them by
exactly that set. Narrowing to a type observed at a handler that is nested in a helper the test reaches
only through another frame is by handler line and is not attributed across functions.

Receipt: not pinned here (the integrator re-pins `catching_clause`, `narrowing_standing` and
`narrowing_candidates` after Wave 4).
Tests: tests/test_exception_narrowing_intent.py (24), plus the policy, swap, canonical-id, monitoring (4
files), census, annotation, filter, engine and guard files: 365 passed under 3.14.6 and under 3.12.13,
PYTHONUNBUFFERED unset. The end-to-end test shows the Detective #103 shape: the two narrowings of
`except Exception` are killed by the test that raises the OTHER observed type, which is now necessary.
With the narrowing disarmed 6 of the 24 fail (the other 18 test the observation and the pure decisions).
ruff clean under pinned 0.16.7.
